atalogue so instead of directly  answering questions we can provide a  catalogue of all the data that's in the  warehouse and they can answer those  questions for themselves so it's really  making them a lot more autonomous saving  our engineers a lot of time and letting  the data engineers focus on the stuff  that really they can only do which is  you know all the hard technical stuff  and you know making these stacks run  really smoothly and so on so this is  sort of our reimagined vision of how  this world looks so there's still this  part that the data engineer owns where  the data gets read out of the source  systems it's passed through pipelines  it's written into the raw data schemas  in the warehouse but then there's some  other thing that the analysts own that  the draw data passes through to put out  transform data all inside the context of  the warehouse right because that's where  they're very comfortable  and finally that transform data can feed  into looker and all these other  processes that that we want to run so in  this scenario the data engineers are  collaborating on this sort of purple  area by providing the platform for these  things to run on but the analysts are  actually the ones doing most of the  implementation of sequel code so this is  the idea behind DBT DBT is an open  source command line based transformation  tool it's designed for all of the sequel  authors at your organization so often at  very small organizations it's used by  like the CEO or somebody who understands  sequel and really wants answers to these  questions and as the organization grows  that can become  a whole bunch of different people who  might understand sequel it's about two  years old we have a little over 250  weekly active projects so it's still  pretty young and not that widely used  but importantly it's a hundred percent  free and open source so from here I'm  gonna kind of give you a whirlwind tour  of all the functionality included in DBT  you can get all of this today by just  running pip install DBT or you can visit  the website and learn a little bit more  so DBT is a developer tool or at least  it acts like one so I think a good  comparison might be something like  terraform there's a command-line  interface that we use in a development  workflow to deploy data models to the  warehouse and this means that your team  members including your analysts end up  working in the terminal and in a text  editor this is a little bit strange for  a lot of analysts at first it's a new  set of tools but we've had a like  amazing success training analysts on how  to use this stack and we found that  they're really eager to get involved  with managing the transformations  they've write sequel transformations  then they execute them using DBT then  they automated test them which we'll  talk about that DBT has some automated  testing functionality built into it and  finally they go through you know QA a  validation deployment to production so  in short when using DBT your analysts  behave like software developers they go  through the same process that software  developers go through just a quick note  on sequel DBT only supports sequel it  doesn't support Python it doesn't  support our it doesn't support Scala or  anything like that and this in a lot of  ways is a limitation of DBT but it turns  out that modern data warehouses can  actually handle a lot so right now it  works with Postgres with redshift and  with snowflake and Google bigquery we  sort of are ready to accept more plugins  to connect to more data bases and really  what we're lacking there is domain  expertise in these specific databases  so for example we would love to be  compatible with link but we just don't  have the local domain expertise to build  that thing out and do it right so if  you're interested in using this and you  don't see your warehouse on this list  come talk to me after it we'd be happy  to hear more about sort of how you can  help us add that to 